WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.702
Secure boot is a defensive startup
concept:

2
00:00:02.782 --> 00:00:07.066
the device should validate that authorized
firmware is allowed to run before handing

3
00:00:07.146 --> 00:00:07.860
control to it.

4
00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:10.558
The details vary by processor, vendor, and
product,

5
00:00:10.638 --> 00:00:12.661
but the engineering idea is consistent.

6
00:00:12.741 --> 00:00:16.966
Startup trust begins before the main
application and depends on a chain of decisions

7
00:00:17.046 --> 00:00:18.236
made in the right order.

8
00:00:18.316 --> 00:00:22.243
A chain of trust usually starts from code
or data that is immutable, protected,

9
00:00:22.323 --> 00:00:24.643
or otherwise controlled by the device
design.

10
00:00:24.723 --> 00:00:28.413
That root is used to check the next stage,
which then checks the next stage,

11
00:00:28.493 --> 00:00:30.694
until the application firmware is reached.

12
00:00:30.774 --> 00:00:33.630
The course does not implement boot code or
cryptography,

13
00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:37.756
but developers should understand the
assumptions their application inherits.

14
00:00:37.836 --> 00:00:42.061
Signed firmware images support integrity
decisions by allowing the device to check

15
00:00:42.141 --> 00:00:45.176
whether an image matches an expected
authorization policy.

16
00:00:45.256 --> 00:00:47.755
The signature check is only one part of
the system.

17
00:00:47.835 --> 00:00:51.107
The design must also address key
protection, image format rules,

18
00:00:51.187 --> 00:00:53.567
version policy, configuration
dependencies,

19
00:00:53.647 --> 00:00:55.730
and what state is trusted during startup.

20
00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:58.547
Failure behavior is as important as
success behavior.

21
00:00:58.627 --> 00:01:02.078
If validation fails, metadata is damaged,
storage is inconsistent,

22
00:01:02.158 --> 00:01:06.502
or a required configuration value is
missing, the device should respond predictably.

23
00:01:06.582 --> 00:01:09.914
That response may involve a recovery mode,
a previous valid image,

24
00:01:09.994 --> 00:01:12.077
a safe service flow, or a controlled stop,

25
00:01:12.157 --> 00:01:15.013
depending on the product and its operating
environment.

26
00:01:15.093 --> 00:01:18.127
Startup integrity depends on configuration
as much as code.

27
00:01:18.207 --> 00:01:21.778
Build settings, fuse or option settings,
debug state, storage layout,

28
00:01:21.858 --> 00:01:26.082
boot order, and recovery permissions can
change the meaning of a secure boot design.

29
00:01:26.162 --> 00:01:29.018
These settings should be reviewed as
release artifacts,

30
00:01:29.098 --> 00:01:31.657
not treated as incidental manufacturing
details.

31
00:01:31.737 --> 00:01:33.522
Testing must cover negative paths.

32
00:01:33.602 --> 00:01:37.351
Teams should test invalid images,
interrupted writes, missing metadata,

33
00:01:37.431 --> 00:01:39.989
incompatible versions, unexpected reset
timing,

34
00:01:40.069 --> 00:01:43.044
and recovery decisions on realistic
hardware conditions.

35
00:01:43.124 --> 00:01:47.051
A startup security feature is only
dependable when the failure cases have been

36
00:01:47.131 --> 00:01:48.500
exercised and documented.
